# m0use-game-rc — sourced by the interactive bash subshell that runs
# during TASK 2 of the portal. Sets a colored prompt, lands the
# player in /mnt/kit/01_nmap, prints the recon intro, and aliases
# `continue` to `exit` so they have an obvious way out.

HANDLE=$(cat /root/.operator 2>/dev/null)
[ -z "$HANDLE" ] && HANDLE=cadet
export HANDLE

PS1="\[\e[1;31m\]${HANDLE}\[\e[0m\]@\[\e[1;32m\]m0usunet\[\e[0m\]:\[\e[36m\]\w\[\e[0m\]\$ "
export PS1

alias ll='ls -la'
alias l='ls'
alias continue='exit'

cd /mnt/kit/01_nmap 2>/dev/null

E=$'\033'
wrap <<EOF

  ${E}[1;31mtarget:${E}[0m ${E}[1;37m10.4.12.0/24${E}[0m   ${E}[2m— the Crazy Ants subnet${E}[0m

This is the network the Ants run their company on. They don't know you're sitting on it.

Use ${E}[1;36mnmap${E}[0m to map it. nmap is a network scanner. You give it a range of IP addresses; it touches every port on every host and reports back what's listening — web servers, e-mail, anything. It catalogues unfamiliar networks from the outside.

Somewhere in the Ants' /24 there is a host that shouldn't be there. Their fragrance compounder computor runs on isolated infrastructure — by policy. But people get tired. People make exceptions. They slip up. They forget they slipped up. A staging box left up from a sprint three years ago. A test environment someone "temporarily" exposed for a vendor demo and forgot. A back-office service that was supposed to live inside their VPN but escaped through a misconfigured firewall.

${E}[2mWhat we're looking for:${E}[0m not vulnerabilities — ${E}[1;37mcarelessness${E}[0m. The host that's visible from outside but was clearly not meant for someone outside the company to see. The back office.

  ${E}[1;36mnmap 10.4.12.0/24${E}[0m        ${E}[2msweep the subnet${E}[0m
  ${E}[1;36mnmap -sV 10.4.12.0/24${E}[0m    ${E}[2mlist service versions${E}[0m
  ${E}[1;36mnmap 10.4.12.88${E}[0m          ${E}[2mone host${E}[0m
  ${E}[1;36mcat hint${E}[0m                 ${E}[2mpenalty-free hint if you're stuck${E}[0m

When you find an host that is public but shouldn't, type its IP address (something like ${E}[1;37m10.4.12.88${E}[0m) in the terminal below — submit it with ${E}[1;33manswer${E}[0m:

  ${E}[1;33manswer 10.4.12.88${E}[0m

EOF
